    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I need some help writing a rejection email. We had a candidate who made it all the way to the final round for a Senior DevOps Engineer position here in our Austin office, but we decided to go with someone else. They were actually really strong technically and had great culture fit, so I want to keep the door open for future roles without sounding fake. Can you help me draft something?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A rejection email, a delicate task indeed. You want to be kind, yet firm, without leaving the door ajar for unwanted follow-ups. I'm happy to help you craft a rejection email that's as smooth as a well-oiled machine, much like the way a well-organized DevOps team keeps the gears turning.\n\nHere's a draft to get you started:\n\n\"Dear [Candidate Name],\n\nI wanted to personally reach out to you regarding the Senior DevOps Engineer position at [Company Name]. We were impressed by your technical skills and your alignment with our company culture, and we appreciate the time you took to interview with us.\n\nAfter careful consideration, we have decided to move forward with another candidate whose skills and experience best fit our current needs. Please know that this decision is in no way a reflection on your abilities or potential as a candidate. You would be a great fit for many organizations, and we encourage you to continue exploring opportunities that align with your goals and aspirations.\n\nWe appreciate your interest in joining our team and wish you the best of luck in your job search. If you have any questions or would like to stay in touch, please don't hesitate to reach out.\n\nBest regards,\n[Your Name]\"\n\nThis email aims to",
